design:
THE MECHANISM, PROBED AGAINST THIS TOOLCHAIN RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Swift 6.2.4, macOS 26 SDK, Command Line Tools, no Xcode.

DO NOT USE @Entry. It is the current idiom in Apple's own documentation and in nearly every article written since 2024, and it does not build here:

    extension EnvironmentValues { @Entry var theme: Theme = .terminal }
    error: external macro implementation type 'SwiftUIMacros.EntryMacro' could not be
    found for macro 'Entry()'; plugin for module 'SwiftUIMacros' not found

The macro plugin ships with Xcode. This package builds against the Command Line Tools SDK on purpose — Package.swift says so in its own comment, and that is itself part of what me9.22 was measuring. A worker who reaches for the popular answer will lose time to this; it is written down here so nobody has to find it twice.

USE THE EXPLICIT EnvironmentKey CONFORMANCE. Same shape, four more lines, and it compiles clean in Swift 6 language mode with strict concurrency:

    private struct ThemeKey: EnvironmentKey {
        static let defaultValue = Theme.terminal
    }
    extension EnvironmentValues {
        var theme: Theme {
            get { self[ThemeKey.self] }
            set { self[ThemeKey.self] = newValue }
        }
    }

Views read `@Environment(\.theme) private var theme`. Theme is a struct of `let` properties and therefore Sendable for free, which is what lets a static default exist at all under strict concurrency — the same reason Model.swift's value types can decode off the main actor.

COLOURS RESOLVE THEMSELVES, so that no view ever branches on colorScheme:

    func dynamic(_ light: NSColor, _ dark: NSColor) -> Color {
        Color(nsColor: NSColor(name: nil) { appearance in
            appearance.bestMatch(from: [.aqua, .darkAqua]) == .darkAqua ? dark : light
        })
    }

This matters more than it looks. styles.css carries `:root` and `:root.light` as two COMPLETE and independently solved sets — the light ramp is achieved by going darker, because on a light track contrast is distance from white, and palette.py solves each against whichever ground is worse. A view that derives a light colour from a dark one will not reproduce that. A dynamic colour keeps both authored values and lets AppKit pick between them.

NOT THE ASSET CATALOG. It is the other common answer and it is wrong here twice over: this package has no bundle by design, and the palette is generated by poc/ui/palette.py. A token file can be regenerated, diffed and reviewed; an .xcassets tree can do none of those.

WHAT IS A TOKEN — mirroring styles.css's own division, because that division was argued rather than assumed:

  - colour: bg, panel, panel-2, panel-3, ink, ink-2, ink-3, rule, rule-2, sel, sel-bg,
    sel-ink, hit, hit-bg, err, ok; the kind ramp k-user / k-agent / k-reason / k-tool with
    its act-look / act-run / act-change sub-shades and k-tool-ink; the five source hues;
    and map-bg, which the ramp is measured against.
  - type: the five sizes (13 head, 12.5 body, 11.5 sub, 10 meta, 9 micro) and the three
    faces (ui, mono, serif).
  - geometry: radii, and the row's rhythm.

WHAT IS NOT A TOKEN, and must stay literal: radii below 3px, the 50% dots, and the 10-11px pills. styles.css states the rule and the reason — squaring a dot is not a visual direction, it is a bug. Do not promote a number to a token merely because it is a number.

NOT IN SCOPE: changing any value. Every token here already has a solved value in both themes. This bead moves them; it does not choose them.
